\fs96 \cf2 Comptime constraints and assertions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Mojo's constraint system lets you express program guarantees that go beyond what the type system provides. This page explains how to use this system effectively.\
A constraint, defined with the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  keyword, represents a precondition for calling a function or instantiating a struct:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  fib\cf5 [\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Int where x \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Int\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa400\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Here the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 fib()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  function requires its 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  parameter to be greater than or equal to 0. The expression 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x >= 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is called a proposition. With the exception of very simple expressions, Mojo doesn't evaluate these propositions literally. Instead, it analyzes them symbolically, tracking a list of propositions that are known to be true in the current scope. Understanding this system of symbolic propositions is key to using constraints effectively.\
Mojo also supports compile-time assertions, which test a proposition at compile time\'97if the assertion evaluates to false, compilation fails:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  \cf5 assert\cf14  x \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 "x must be greater than or equal to 0."\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Defining constraints\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 You can use constraints in the following contexts:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
In the parameter list of a function or struct, to constrain the values that you can bind to one or more parameters.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def fib[x: Int where x >= 0]() -> Int:
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 A constraint can involve multiple parameters, as long as the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause comes after the parameters:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def subspan[start: Int, end: Int where end > start](self) -> Self:
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
In a method declaration, to constrain the availability of the method based on parameters of the parent struct.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 def sort() where conforms_to(Self.T, Comparable):
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls4\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
In the trait conformance list for a struct, to declare that a struct conforms to a trait only when certain conditions are met.\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 struct MyContainer[T: AnyType](Copyable where conforms_to(T, Copyable)):
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 You'll use this form, called conditional trait conformance, when defining generic types. For details, see the section on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://docs.modular.com/stable/mojo/manual/generics/#conditional-trait-conformance"}}{\fldrslt \cf5 \ul \ulc5 conditional trait conformances}}.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Symbolic propositions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The constraint system works with symbolic propositions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  first\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int where size \cf5 >\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ](\cf14 array\cf5 :\cf14  InlineArray\cf5 [\cf14 _\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 ])\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  array\cf5 .\cf14 T\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 In this case, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 size > 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is a proposition that the constraint system tracks.\
By analogy, when you annotate a type on an argument you ask the compiler to ensure callers only pass that type. When you annotate a constraint on a function, you ask the compiler to ensure that the proposition is true at the call site.\
But the compiler can't test every proposition at every call site without running or interpreting unbounded amounts of code, which would vastly expand compile times. Instead, callers need to explicitly introduce "knowledge" into the constraint system.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Introducing knowledge\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The compiler tracks knowledge by scopes: each scope contains a set of known true propositions, also known as "knowledge," and nested scopes accumulate knowledge from outer scopes.\
There are four ways to introduce knowledge to the system:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Inside a struct declaration, all constraints declared on the struct are known, because concrete instances of the struct type can only be created when the proposition is true:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 struct\cf14  \cf19 List\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int where size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf6 # Knowledge base:\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf6 # - `size >= 0`\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Inside a function, all constraints declared on the function are known, because callers can only call the function if they guarantee the constraint holds:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  create_list\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int where size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf6 # Knowledge base:\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf6 # - `size >= 0`\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
def\cf14  create_list\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]\cf14  where size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf6 # Knowledge base:\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf6 # - `size >= 0`\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Inside a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  condition is known, because the code within the body is only instantiated if the condition is true:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf6 # Knowledge base:\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf6 # - `size >= 0`\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  size\cf5 .\cf14 is_even\cf5 ():\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf6 # Knowledge base:\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf6 # - `size >= 0`\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
    \cf6 # - `size.is_even()`\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa160\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
After a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , the asserted condition is known, because the compiler won't instantiate a function if a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  condition is false. So any code after it is only instantiated if the assertion didn't fire:\uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  \cf5 assert\cf14  size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf6 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
# Knowledge base:\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cf6 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
# - `size >= 0`\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf14 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf5 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
comptime\cf14  \cf5 assert\cf14  size\cf5 .\cf14 is_even\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf6 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
# Knowledge base:\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cf6 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
# - `size >= 0`\cf14 \cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0\cf6 \cb13 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
# - `size.is_even()`\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0\cf10 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\uc0\u8232 \cf14 \cb13 \uc0\u8232 
\fs32 \cf16 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf17 \uc0\u8232 \cf16 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Satisfying constraints with knowledge\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Any time you call a function that has constraints, the system inspects the set of known-true propositions at the call site and determines whether the known set of propositions is a superset of the required set of propositions declared on the callee.\
The constraint system treats all propositions symbolically: it doesn't know or care what the expression means and doesn't interpret the code. With a few very simple exceptions (discussed later), the system doesn't perform symbolic math on your behalf. Instead, it treats these propositions as opaque and only uses knowledge you've explicitly introduced in the calling scope.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # We have a function that wants a non-empty list.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  print_first\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int where size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 1\cf5 ](\cf14 l\cf5 :\cf14  InlineArray\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # A wrapper that wants a size >= 2 list.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  print_first_two\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int where size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 2\cf5 ](\cf14 l\cf5 :\cf14  InlineArray\cf5 [\cf14 Int\cf5 ,\cf14  size\cf5 ]):\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13      \cf6 # Error: invalid call to 'print_first': lacking evidence to prove\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13      \cf6 # correctness\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     print_first\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ](\cf14 l\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf6 # ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Mojo checks a constraints without knowing all the call sites, and these expressions can reference symbolic parameter values-for example 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is_prime(x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  where 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is unknown. Because of this, Mojo can't interpret 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is_prime()
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for all possible values of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , nor can it make logical deductions. For example if 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is_prime(x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x > 2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  are true, it can't deduce that 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 is_odd(x)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  must also be true.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Compile-time assertions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  to introduce a known-true proposition at a specific point in the code:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 comptime\cf14  \cf5 assert\cf14  x \cf5 >\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 "x must be greater than 0."\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The message is optional. If the condition evaluates to false at compile time, compilation fails and the compiler shows the message (or a generic message if none is specified).\
Mojo adds the asserted condition to the list of "known true" propositions for any code following the assertion.\
Constraints and assertions serve complementary roles: a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause exposes a requirement that callers must prove, and 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is one way to satisfy that requirement.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Limited evaluation of propositions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 In general, it's safe to assume that the constraint system doesn't evaluate propositions directly, and that all knowledge needs to be provided explicitly. However, the constraint system does apply a very limited amount of "smartness" for very common cases. These are provided as a convenience and should not be treated as the norm.\
The goal is to provide a consistent, predictable experience so users can recognize these patterns as they become more familiar with the system.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Simple implication\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 A known proposition of the form 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 A and B
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  can satisfy a requirement of 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 A
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by itself (or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 B
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  by itself), even though symbolically they aren't identical propositions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  create_even_list\cf5 [\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   size\cf5 :\cf14  Int where size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf14  \cf5 and\cf14  size\cf5 .\cf14 is_even\cf5 ()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf5 \cb13 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf6 # No need to individually assert that `size >= 0`.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 return\cf14  create_list\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Similarly, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 A
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  implies 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 A or B
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for any 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 B
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Canonicalization\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 Sometimes there is more than one way to write the same expression. The system always simplifies expressions into a normal form internally, so expressions that aren't identical on the surface may be seen as identical by the system.\
The following are self-explanatory (assume 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x: Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ):\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
x > 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  == 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 x >= 1
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
x >= 2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  == 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 not (x < 2)
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls6\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
x + x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  == 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 2 * x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 There's a special case for function calls. When invoking functions as part of a proposition, the system treats the entire function call as opaque. Only two identical function calls are the same.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf19 is_even\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 )\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Bool\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 return\cf14  x \cf5 %\cf14  \cf15 2\cf14  \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf15 0\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  needs_even\cf5 [\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Int where is_even\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 )]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 pass\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  forward_even_bad\cf5 [\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Int where x \cf5 %\cf14  \cf15 2\cf14  \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf6 # ERROR: Needs evidence for `is_even(x)`.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   needs_even\cf5 [\cf14 x\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  forward_even_good\cf5 [\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Int where is_even\cf5 (\cf14 x\cf5 )]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf6 # SUCCESS\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   needs_even\cf5 [\cf14 x\cf5 ]\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb20 Builtin functions\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa127\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \cb20 Some functions in the standard library can be evaluated in 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 where
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  clauses. These functions implement simple operations on core types (for example, 
\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf10 \cb21 Int
\fs28 \cf2 \cb20  numerics), which are known to the compiler. These functions and can be inlined into the calling expression when called in a parameter context.\cb1 \
\cb20 However, there's no way to identify these builtin functions without looking at the source code, and whether a given function is builtin may change without notice.\cb1 \
\cb20 If you need a predicate that works transparently rather than opaquely, consider implementing it as a parametric comptime value instead, which is always inlined.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs24\fsmilli12250 \cf5 \cb21 comptime\cf14  is_even\cf5 [\cf14 x\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]:\cf14  Bool \cf5 =\cf14  x \cf5 %\cf14  \cf15 2\cf14  \cf5 ==\cf14  \cf15 0\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Context-free folding\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 This is more of an extreme case of canonicalization than a separate category, but may catch people by surprise.\
Certain primitive operations on constants can be canonicalized into a new constant. For example:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
1 + 1
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  == 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
4 % 2
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  == 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 0
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\ls7\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
1 == 1
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  == 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 True
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This extends to expressions with a mix of constants and non-constants:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls8\ilvl0
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
1 + x + 1
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  == 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 2 + x
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Concretely, this means you may omit explicitly providing knowledge for propositions that are simple operations on constants:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  \cf19 create_my_list\cf5 ()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf15 2\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf6 # No need to provide evidence for `2 >= 0`.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 return\cf14  create_list\cf5 [\cf15 2\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Best practices\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 The examples here focus on functions (declaring constraints when writing functions and satisfying constraints when calling functions), but the tips generalize to other forms of constraints such as struct parameter constraints and conditional trait conformances.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Writing functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When writing a function, how do you decide whether a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause is right for you?\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 1. Deciding when to use constraints\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Q1: Does your function handle the entire domain of its input types?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you can write your function so that it returns a result or throws an error on all inputs, you don't need to care about constraints at all. Just write your function as usual.\
For example, the following functions do not need constraints since they're guaranteed to return or throw:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  create_list_opt\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Optional\cf5 [\cf14 List\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  size \cf5 <\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  \cf15 None\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 return\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  create_list_raise\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]()\cf14  raises \cf5 ->\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  size \cf5 <\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 raise\cf14  Error\cf5 (\cf18 "negative size!"\cf5 )\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 return\cf14  \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Constraints are only for cases where you don't want to check for exceptional cases at execution time.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls9\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Constraints ask the type checker to rule out exceptional cases so that a type-checked program guarantees you (as the function author) don't need to handle these cases in your function body using run-time resources.\
\ls9\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
As always, enforcing static guarantees isn't free. You're trading off run-time error checking logic for compile-time proof writing. In the absence of hard limits that prevent you from error checking at run time, it comes down to your preference for user experience.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Q2: Is this limitation a central concept of your code?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If the condition represents a concept that is central to your code, a dedicated type may be easier than sprinkling 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  everywhere.\
Examples:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls10\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A SIMD library that needs to represent SIMD width values, which aren't arbitrary integers.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A filesystem library that needs to represent paths that follow specific format rules.\
\ls10\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A network library that needs to represent port numbers, which must be in the valid range.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 This is a good approach because the constraint is proven once (at construction) and the refined value can be passed around unconstrained until it needs to be disassembled. Your APIs stay simpler: fewer 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clauses, fewer repeated asserts.\
A new type doesn't come for free though, as it's no longer freely interoperable with the original type. Make sure that the semantics are distinct enough to warrant the extra code. For example, writing a new type usually means implementing dunder methods corresponding to common operators (addition, subtraction, equality, and so on), which preserve the semantics of the new type.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Q3: Is the constraint understandable and provable by the caller?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If yes, use constraints.\
Make the condition part of the function's contract so that callers must prove it.\
This tends to be the right choice when:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls11\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The condition is a user-facing requirement that the user can understand.\
\ls11\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Callers typically already need to handle the "bad" case themselves (for example, they may already have a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  that branches on this condition).\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  take_prefix\cf5 [\cf14 n\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf19 len\cf5 :\cf14  Int where \cf15 0\cf14  \cf5 <=\cf14  n \cf5 <=\cf14  \cf19 len\cf5 ](...)\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  \cf5 ...:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 One practical heuristic: if a user can read the function signature and immediately understand what they did wrong when the constraint fails, 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is likely the right tool.\
If the constraint is not understandable or not provable by the user, use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 abort
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
If the "bad case" isn't something users would understand, adding a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  constraint only causes more confusion, as users can't reasonably prove it themselves.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls12\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
This typically happens when your library returns a value that has some internal constraints on it and expects users to pass it back with those constraints. For example, a communication library passes a device handle to the user as an 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 , which has properties that are only known internally (for example, non-negative, special bits). Library APIs accepting this handle can't expect the user to prove these.\
\ls12\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
There are usually more type-safe ways to achieve the same thing, so only do this if you don't care about type safety. For example, a more type-safe approach is to introduce a new type for the device handle so users are less likely to accidentally pass another 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 Int
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or modify the returned value unexpectedly.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Summary\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls13\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use a dedicated type when the condition is a common refinement that should be proven once and reused everywhere.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  when the condition is a user-understandable precondition.\
\ls13\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Use 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  or 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 abort
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  for internal inconsistencies in your library where a user can't do anything with the failure.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs48 \cf2 Calling functions\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 When calling a function, how do you show proof that you've satisfied the constraint?\
This is where the constraint system actively guides you into handling exceptional cases.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 {{\NeXTGraphic Attachment.png \width320 \height320 \appleattachmentpadding0 \appleembedtype0 \appleaqc
}¬}\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs27\fsmilli13600 \cf2 Figure 2. Using constrained APIs\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Q1: Do the constrained parameters come from a parent parameter list?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If the constrained parameter you're passing is from the function's parameter list, or is a parameter on the enclosing struct, you're basically "forwarding" a value from one parameter list to another. Go to Q2.\
If not, you computed the value inside the function body, and you're in "construction" territory. Go to Q3.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Q2: Does the same limitation apply to your function?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If you're forwarding a constrained parameter into another constrained API, it's usually a hint to propagate the same requirement onto your own function.\
For example, to propagate the constraint to your callers:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  create_list\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int where size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  List\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf6 \cb13 # This function is also only meaningful when size >= 0.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb13 # Make that part of the contract too.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  create_list_and_fill\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int where size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ]():\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 comptime\cf14  xs \cf5 =\cf14  create_list\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 ...\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 But if you want your function to accept a wider input domain, your job is to handle both cases explicitly using a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 .\
For example, narrow the domain by checking before the call:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  create_list_and_fill\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Optional\cf5 [\cf14 List\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  needs_nonneg\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 else:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  \cf15 None\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 Q3: Do you know that the parameter already satisfies this condition?\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf2 If the parameter didn't come from a parent parameter list, it must have been computed in the body.\
Decide whether the desired constraint holds by construction.\
If it does, indicate this to the constraint system via a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 . Note that this is you explicitly taking over the burden of proof.\
Don't be afraid to write these asserts. The symbolic nature of the constraint system means that it is conservative\'97logical deductions that are obvious to you aren't always "obvious" to it. Adding 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  is not a code smell, but rather an inseparable part of working within the constraint system.\
For example, given a computed parameter that is always valid:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # The constraint on hi & lo guarantees a valid size.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb13 # Introduce this piece of knowledge explicitly.\cf14 \cb1 \
\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf14 \cb13   \cf5 comptime\cf14  size \cf5 =\cf14  hi \cf5 -\cf14  lo\cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 assert\cf14  size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 ,\cf14  \cf18 "span is guaranteed non-negative"\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 return\cf14  create_list\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf2 But if the constraint does not necessarily hold, insert a 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  and handle both cases explicitly.\
For example, a computed parameter that may be invalid:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf6 \cb13 # This version does NOT have a constraint on its inputs.\cf14 \cb1 \
\cf6 \cb13 # Branch on the computation to handle both cases.\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf5 \cb13 def\cf14  create_list_from_span\cf5 [\cf14 lo\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ,\cf14  hi\cf5 :\cf14  Int\cf5 ]()\cf14  \cf5 ->\cf14  Optional\cf5 [\cf14 List\cf5 [\cf14 hi \cf5 -\cf14  lo\cf5 ]]:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 comptime\cf14  size \cf5 =\cf14  hi \cf5 -\cf14  lo\cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 comptime\cf14  \cf5 if\cf14  size \cf5 >=\cf14  \cf15 0\cf5 :\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  create_list\cf5 [\cf14 size\cf5 ]()\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13   \cf5 else:\cf14 \cb1 \
\cb13     \cf5 return\cf14  \cf15 None\cf14 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf16 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0
\cf17 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa800\partightenfactor0

\fs64 \cf2 Summary\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Constraints are part of the API contract.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
A 
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 where
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1  clause is a precondition that the API author requires the caller to prove.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The compiler doesn't automatically derive evidence for callers.\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u9702 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
The caller must explicitly provide evidence that the precondition is always satisfied.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl0\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
If a caller gets a "lacking evidence" error, they can:\
\pard\tx940\tx1440\pardeftab720\li1440\fi-1440\partightenfactor0
\ls14\ilvl1\cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	i	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Add a constraint to push the requirement onto their own callers.\
\ls14\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	ii	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Branch on the condition (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime if
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
\ls14\ilvl1\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	iii	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Assert an invariant (
\fs28 \cf10 \cb12 comptime assert
\fs32 \cf2 \cb1 ).\
